WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.100
Welcome to Day 5: Household Operations, Vendors and Risk Management.

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:10.175
We'll start with running the household, build a Home Binder,

00:00:10.175 --> 00:00:12.862
then move on to vendors and procurement.

00:00:12.862 --> 00:00:17.125
The biggest block today is risk management, followed by travel risk,

00:00:17.125 --> 00:00:21.325
and we'll close with a mid-point review of how the course is going for you.

00:00:22.075 --> 00:00:26.050
By the end of today, you'll be able to run household operations like a small

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:29.762
business, keep a Home Binder the family can rely on,

00:00:29.762 --> 00:00:34.725
manage vendors and contracts, and apply a real risk-management framework.

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:39.575
Section 1 of 6: Running the Household.

00:00:39.575 --> 00:00:42.025
We'll treat the household like a small business,

00:00:42.025 --> 00:00:46.225
then look at household staff, home maintenance and personal support.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:52.475
A busy household runs smoothly when you treat it like a small business.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:56.287
Think about how many people touch a busy family's life:

00:00:56.287 --> 00:01:00.525
a nanny, a housekeeper, contractors, school, doctors.

00:01:00.525 --> 00:01:04.675
If nobody's coordinating, everyone calls Sarah or Elias.

00:01:05.475 --> 00:01:10.225
First, make yourself the main contact for family, staff and contractors.

00:01:10.225 --> 00:01:13.400
Then, set up simple routines for things that repeat,

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.450
like bills, staff schedules and maintenance.

00:01:18.375 --> 00:01:22.850
After that, review every so often what's working and what keeps slipping.

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:26.163
Finally, write down the household's standing decisions,

00:01:26.163 --> 00:01:29.000
so things run the same way when you're not there.

00:01:29.775 --> 00:01:31.925
Here's a situation to work through.

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:34.375
You've just taken over the Thornes' household.

00:01:34.375 --> 00:01:39.038
There's no shared calendar, the budget is 'in Sarah's head' and vendor numbers are

00:01:39.038 --> 00:01:41.175
scattered across three phones.

00:01:41.175 --> 00:01:44.300
What do you build in your first week, and in what order?

00:01:46.225 --> 00:01:50.875
If you don't decide before a subscription renews, auto-renew decides for you.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:54.413
Recurring admin comes in three flavors.

00:01:54.413 --> 00:01:58.038
Utilities, like power and water, which can never lapse.

00:01:58.038 --> 00:02:02.525
Purchasing, for work and for home, where it's easy to buy something twice or forget

00:02:02.525 --> 00:02:03.200
it.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.650
And subscriptions, which most people sign up for once and never think about again,

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.150
until the charge shows up on the statement.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:16.350
First, put every utility payment on a calendar with its real due date.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:20.050
Next, keep a simple record of what's been bought and when.

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:24.350
Then, note every subscription's renewal date the day you sign up.

00:02:26.275 --> 00:02:31.200
After that, before each renewal, actively decide: keep it or cancel it.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.800
Finally, give all three the same attention, even though none feels urgent on any

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:37.000
given day.

00:02:37.775 --> 00:02:38.912
Think about this.

00:02:38.912 --> 00:02:42.187
You discover the Thornes pay for three streaming services,

00:02:42.187 --> 00:02:45.800
two meal-kit subscriptions and a gym nobody uses.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.925
How would you review them, and what would you bring to Sarah?

00:02:50.925 --> 00:02:54.325
Write down what's expected of household staff from day one,

00:02:54.325 --> 00:02:56.612
because verbal agreements drift.

00:02:56.612 --> 00:03:01.600
By the time there's a disagreement, nobody remembers the original deal the same way.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:07.775
Managing household staff is mostly coordination, not bossing people around.

00:03:07.775 --> 00:03:12.950
And a schedule that only works if someone stays late every week isn't sustainable.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:17.888
First, keep a short written description of each role:

00:03:17.888 --> 00:03:20.925
hours, main duties and who they report to.

00:03:20.925 --> 00:03:24.237
Next, keep a shared calendar of everyone's schedule.

00:03:24.237 --> 00:03:29.850
Then, have a short, regular check-in, even monthly, to catch small problems early.

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:34.650
Finally, keep emergency contacts and basic medical information on file for each

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:35.925
staff member.

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:37.875
Think about this.

00:03:37.875 --> 00:03:42.262
The nanny tells you, only when you ask, that she's been working an extra hour every

00:03:42.262 --> 00:03:45.662
evening for three weeks because the Thornes come home late.

00:03:45.662 --> 00:03:46.900
She never mentioned it.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:48.000
What do you do?

00:03:49.975 --> 00:03:55.000
Most big, expensive repairs started as a small one that nobody scheduled.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:00.150
Home maintenance comes in two kinds: routine jobs you can plan,

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:02.675
like servicing the heating or clearing gutters,

00:04:02.675 --> 00:04:05.250
and surprises, like a burst pipe.

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:09.450
If you treat them the same way, the routine ones get forgotten and the surprises

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:10.900
become panics.

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:16.850
First, build a maintenance calendar for every system with a recommended service

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:17.850
schedule.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:21.487
Next, keep a list of trusted tradespeople for each specialty.

00:04:21.487 --> 00:04:25.937
Then, for any repair, get the scope and cost in writing before work starts,

00:04:25.937 --> 00:04:27.825
even with someone you trust.

00:04:27.825 --> 00:04:31.062
Finally, keep warranties and manuals in the Home Binder,

00:04:31.062 --> 00:04:34.425
so 'is this still covered?' takes seconds.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.450
Here's a situation to work through.

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:41.862
At 9 p.m., the housekeeper texts that there's water coming through the kitchen

00:04:41.862 --> 00:04:42.838
ceiling.

00:04:42.838 --> 00:04:49.925
Your approval limit for repairs is $1,000, and the emergency plumber quotes $1,800.

00:04:49.925 --> 00:04:51.350
What do you do tonight?

00:04:53.275 --> 00:04:56.862
Your memory can't hold every gift, errand and event.

00:04:56.862 --> 00:05:00.987
A shared tracker can, with a deadline and an owner for each one.

00:05:00.987 --> 00:05:05.475
Handling them on the fly works for a week, and then something important slips.

00:05:06.275 --> 00:05:08.937
First, log a request the moment it comes in.

00:05:08.937 --> 00:05:12.550
Then, give each item a clear owner, even if that's you.

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:19.850
After that, check the tracker regularly, so nothing arrives as a last-minute panic.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:23.037
Finally, treat it like any other household system:

00:05:23.037 --> 00:05:25.475
it only works if you actually use it.

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:27.275
Think about this.

00:05:27.275 --> 00:05:31.425
You're choosing this year's holiday gift for Elias's biggest client.

00:05:31.425 --> 00:05:34.025
Last year's gift was a bottle of wine.

00:05:34.025 --> 00:05:36.150
The client has since stopped drinking.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:38.200
What does your tracker need to show?

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:42.938
Section 2 of 6: The Home Binder.

00:05:42.938 --> 00:05:45.725
What goes in it, how to keep it secure and current,

00:05:45.725 --> 00:05:48.000
and the digital tools that can hold it.

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:54.900
The home binder exists for one moment: when someone else needs critical information

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:56.812
and you're not there to tell them.

00:05:56.812 --> 00:06:00.662
Picture the alarm going off while the nanny is home with the children,

00:06:00.662 --> 00:06:02.275
and you're unreachable.

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:05.138
A good binder has four parts.

00:06:05.138 --> 00:06:09.525
How the house runs, with codes, manuals and utility accounts.

00:06:09.525 --> 00:06:13.188
Family and medical details, like doctors and allergies.

00:06:13.188 --> 00:06:15.850
Pointers to financial and legal documents.

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:17.800
And emergency contacts.

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:21.950
First, build the household section.

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:24.375
Next, add family and medical.

00:06:24.375 --> 00:06:28.275
Then, add financial and legal information as pointers only:

00:06:28.275 --> 00:06:31.512
where to find the policy, not the policy number itself.

00:06:31.512 --> 00:06:34.862
After that, organize the emergency contacts.

00:06:34.862 --> 00:06:39.950
Finally, test it: could a substitute or a paramedic use it without calling you?

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:42.638
Here's a way to test yours.

00:06:42.638 --> 00:06:45.375
The nanny has to find the pediatrician's number,

00:06:45.375 --> 00:06:50.175
the alarm company and the location of the water shut-off valve in under two minutes,

00:06:50.175 --> 00:06:51.612
without calling you.

00:06:51.612 --> 00:06:55.275
What would make that possible, and what usually makes it fail?

00:06:57.225 --> 00:06:59.538
Should the binder be paper or digital?

00:06:59.538 --> 00:07:02.525
Paper works when the power or internet is down.

00:07:02.525 --> 00:07:05.700
Digital is easy to update and reach from anywhere.

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:10.225
Most households need both: a digital master, with a printed copy for real

00:07:10.225 --> 00:07:11.525
emergencies.

00:07:12.325 --> 00:07:16.088
First, choose the format based on how it will actually be used.

00:07:16.088 --> 00:07:19.912
Then, never write sensitive numbers, like account numbers,

00:07:19.912 --> 00:07:22.900
passwords or ID numbers, in the binder.

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:25.225
Write down where to find them instead.

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:30.225
After that, update it the moment something changes.

00:07:30.225 --> 00:07:34.550
Finally, check now and then that nobody has written a sensitive number in by

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:35.600
mistake.

00:07:36.375 --> 00:07:37.562
Think about this.

00:07:37.562 --> 00:07:41.450
You're reviewing the Thornes' binder and find the safe combination and a bank

00:07:41.450 --> 00:07:44.250
account number written on the finance page.

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:48.025
What do you change, and where does that information go instead?

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:54.725
A simple, well-organized tool beats a clever one nobody else can use.

00:07:55.525 --> 00:08:00.275
First, if the household already uses a shared workspace, build the binder there.

00:08:00.275 --> 00:08:04.250
Next, use the same four section headings as a paper binder.

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:06.838
Then, set permissions per section.

00:08:06.838 --> 00:08:11.263
A driver needs the household section, not the family's medical details.

00:08:11.263 --> 00:08:16.450
Finally, print or export a backup regularly, in case the tool is down.

00:08:17.225 --> 00:08:19.538
Here's a situation to work through.

00:08:19.538 --> 00:08:22.675
The Thornes' previous housekeeper left three months ago,

00:08:22.675 --> 00:08:25.800
and you discover she can still log into the digital binder,

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:28.075
which includes the alarm codes.

00:08:28.075 --> 00:08:31.750
What do you do today, and what process stops it happening again?

00:08:33.675 --> 00:08:37.250
Section 3 of 6: Vendors and Procurement.

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:41.275
Choosing and managing vendors, negotiating and reading contracts,

00:08:41.275 --> 00:08:43.600
and what to do when a vendor falls through.

00:08:45.575 --> 00:08:49.475
Choosing a vendor well is what stops vendors from failing you later.

00:08:49.475 --> 00:08:52.800
It's a sequence, and each step protects the next.

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:55.775
First, look at more than one option.

00:08:55.775 --> 00:09:01.075
Next, compare more than price: reliability, response time and contract terms.

00:09:01.075 --> 00:09:06.175
Then, put it in writing: a real contract or statement of work, not a handshake.

00:09:06.175 --> 00:09:09.975
Finally, keep managing it: track performance and renewal dates,

00:09:09.975 --> 00:09:11.675
and have a backup ready.

00:09:12.425 --> 00:09:13.713
Try this one.

00:09:13.713 --> 00:09:17.775
A tree-removal company offers to do urgent work tomorrow for cash,

00:09:17.775 --> 00:09:19.275
with no written quote.

00:09:19.275 --> 00:09:21.600
They seem friendly and experienced.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:24.400
What do you check before they step onto the property?

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:31.162
Paying a vendor's invoices on time doesn't mean the vendor is doing a good job.

00:09:31.162 --> 00:09:33.025
The work doesn't stop at signing.

00:09:33.025 --> 00:09:35.225
You keep watching whether they deliver.

00:09:37.175 --> 00:09:40.100
First, compare real options before committing.

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:43.363
Next, read the actual terms, not just the price.

00:09:43.363 --> 00:09:46.500
Then, after signing, check that they're performing.

00:09:48.425 --> 00:09:52.375
After that, track renewal dates like any other recurring admin.

00:09:52.375 --> 00:09:55.650
Finally, have a backup ready before you need one.

00:09:56.425 --> 00:09:57.600
Think about this.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:01.100
The Thornes' pool service has been paid on time every month,

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:04.388
but the pool has turned green twice this summer.

00:10:04.388 --> 00:10:08.475
What should ongoing vendor management have caught, and what do you do now?

00:10:10.425 --> 00:10:15.213
Your best negotiating tool is a real alternative lined up before you start.

00:10:15.213 --> 00:10:19.325
A vendor who knows you have no other option has no reason to budge.

00:10:19.325 --> 00:10:23.488
And most vendors' standard contracts are more flexible than people think,

00:10:23.488 --> 00:10:27.875
especially on payment, cancellation and service guarantees.

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:31.488
First, know what matters most to you, in order:

00:10:31.488 --> 00:10:34.975
price, timing, flexibility or guarantees.

00:10:34.975 --> 00:10:38.725
Next, ask for better terms on at least one thing besides price,

00:10:38.725 --> 00:10:41.100
like a shorter cancellation notice.

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:44.625
Then, get everything you agree into the written contract.

00:10:44.625 --> 00:10:49.850
Finally, renegotiate at renewal instead of letting it roll over automatically.

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:51.863
Think about this.

00:10:51.863 --> 00:10:56.950
A vendor the family has used happily for two years sends a renewal with a 15 percent

00:10:56.950 --> 00:10:59.363
increase and no explanation.

00:10:59.363 --> 00:11:02.012
You have one untested alternative.

00:11:02.012 --> 00:11:04.200
How do you approach the conversation?

00:11:06.125 --> 00:11:10.775
You'll handle contracts every week, and the costly mistakes hide in a few clauses

00:11:10.775 --> 00:11:12.225
with dates in them.

00:11:12.975 --> 00:11:15.188
We don't decide what a contract should say.

00:11:15.188 --> 00:11:16.875
That's the attorney's job.

00:11:16.875 --> 00:11:19.275
But we do need to recognize the parts.

00:11:19.275 --> 00:11:23.025
Most contracts have the same building blocks: who the parties are,

00:11:23.025 --> 00:11:26.800
what's being provided, payment, how long it lasts and whether it renews,

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:30.400
how it can be ended, confidentiality, indemnification,

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:33.650
which is who covers whose losses, limits on liability,

00:11:33.650 --> 00:11:37.300
insurance, which state's law applies, and the signatures.

00:11:39.250 --> 00:11:42.150
First, we read the first page for the parties and date,

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:46.600
then find the term: start, end and automatic renewal.

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:50.925
Next, we find every notice period and put a reminder in the calendar before each

00:11:50.925 --> 00:11:51.713
one.

00:11:51.713 --> 00:11:56.025
Then we note payment terms and scope, so invoices can be checked.

00:11:57.950 --> 00:12:01.375
After that, we write a one-page summary for the file.

00:12:01.375 --> 00:12:04.875
Finally, the contract goes to the attorney before signing,

00:12:04.875 --> 00:12:07.300
with anything unusual flagged.

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:10.488
Pause the video and work this one out.

00:12:10.488 --> 00:12:15.525
A three-year catering contract says it 'renews automatically for successive one-year

00:12:15.525 --> 00:12:20.963
terms unless either party gives 90 days' written notice.' It started March 1,

00:12:20.963 --> 00:12:22.388
2024.

00:12:22.388 --> 00:12:26.200
When is the last day to give notice, and what goes in the calendar?

00:12:28.100 --> 00:12:32.713
When a vendor lets you down, the worst thing you can do is try to fix it quietly

00:12:32.713 --> 00:12:34.638
while the clock runs down.

00:12:34.638 --> 00:12:38.800
The best thing is to be the first to say what's happened and what you propose to do

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:39.850
about it.

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:43.025
First, tell the household right away.

00:12:43.025 --> 00:12:46.575
Next, offer specific alternatives in the same message.

00:12:46.575 --> 00:12:50.600
Then, go to your pre-checked backup vendor, not a cold search.

00:12:52.525 --> 00:12:56.088
After that, confirm the backup can actually deliver in time,

00:12:56.088 --> 00:12:58.675
so one failure doesn't become two.

00:12:58.675 --> 00:13:02.525
Finally, once it's resolved, update the vendor records.

00:13:03.275 --> 00:13:05.000
Take a second with this one.

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:10.350
The caterer for Sarah Thorne's dinner party for 20 cancels at 2 p.m. on the day.

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:14.750
What do you do in the first five minutes, and what does your message to Sarah say?

00:13:16.700 --> 00:13:20.213
Some vendors see private household or business information.

00:13:20.213 --> 00:13:24.600
For them, the NDA gets signed first, and access comes second.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:26.475
But it doesn't end at signing.

00:13:27.275 --> 00:13:31.850
First, decide which vendors really need an NDA, based on what they'll see.

00:13:31.850 --> 00:13:36.475
Next, keep a simple record of who has signed, when and what it covers.

00:13:36.475 --> 00:13:40.200
Finally, check it again whenever the vendor's work expands.

00:13:40.975 --> 00:13:42.125
Think about this.

00:13:42.125 --> 00:13:46.788
The home IT technician's work grows from setting up Wi-Fi to reorganizing the

00:13:46.788 --> 00:13:48.738
family's personal files.

00:13:48.738 --> 00:13:52.025
The original NDA only covered network setup.

00:13:52.025 --> 00:13:53.100
What do you do?

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:58.150
Section 4 of 6: Risk Management.

00:13:58.150 --> 00:14:01.400
We'll start with the framework and the four core risk strategies,

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:05.300
then work through the specific risks an EA or PA manages,

00:14:05.300 --> 00:14:07.375
like information security.

00:14:09.350 --> 00:14:12.225
Risk in a household falls into four groups.

00:14:12.225 --> 00:14:15.925
Money risk, like a missed insurance premium or fraud.

00:14:15.925 --> 00:14:19.312
Legal risk, like someone getting hurt on the property.

00:14:19.312 --> 00:14:22.600
Operational risk, like a vendor who doesn't show up.

00:14:22.600 --> 00:14:27.300
And reputation risk, like a family matter ending up on social media.

00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:31.713
The trick is that real incidents rarely stay in just one group,

00:14:31.713 --> 00:14:33.300
so look at all of them.

00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:39.525
First, for financial risk, keep an eye on renewals and confirm payments went

00:14:39.525 --> 00:14:40.475
through.

00:14:40.475 --> 00:14:46.050
Next, for legal risk, notice injury, contract or staff issues as they come up.

00:14:46.050 --> 00:14:51.125
Then, for operational risk, have backup plans ready before something goes wrong.

00:14:53.050 --> 00:14:57.400
After that, for reputational risk, make discretion a daily habit.

00:14:57.400 --> 00:15:00.625
Finally, when something happens, check it against all four,

00:15:00.625 --> 00:15:02.500
not just the obvious one.

00:15:03.275 --> 00:15:05.438
Here's a situation to work through.

00:15:05.438 --> 00:15:10.200
A delivery driver slips on the Thornes' icy driveway and posts about it on social

00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:11.225
media.

00:15:11.225 --> 00:15:15.825
Map it to every risk category it touches, and say what you'd do first.

00:15:17.750 --> 00:15:20.675
There are only four ways to handle a risk: avoid it,

00:15:20.675 --> 00:15:23.350
reduce it, insure it or accept it.

00:15:24.150 --> 00:15:28.025
First, avoid: if a risk can reasonably be skipped, skip it.

00:15:28.025 --> 00:15:31.663
Next, reduce: if you can't avoid it, lower it, with alarms,

00:15:31.663 --> 00:15:33.800
training or safer driving.

00:15:33.800 --> 00:15:38.550
Then, transfer: if the money at stake is big, move it to an insurer.

00:15:40.475 --> 00:15:44.600
After that, retain: if insuring costs more than the risk itself,

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:46.425
accept it on purpose.

00:15:46.425 --> 00:15:49.563
Finally, choose the approach for each specific risk,

00:15:49.563 --> 00:15:52.350
rather than using the same one for everything.

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:55.350
Pause the video and try this.

00:15:55.350 --> 00:16:00.413
Match a strategy to each risk: a leaky pool fence with young children in the house,

00:16:00.413 --> 00:16:05.650
a $3,000 painting, the family's annual trip to Europe and a scratch-prone rental

00:16:05.650 --> 00:16:06.525
car.

00:16:08.425 --> 00:16:09.250
Quick check.

00:16:09.250 --> 00:16:13.638
A family purchases homeowners insurance to cover potential losses from fire or

00:16:13.638 --> 00:16:14.550
theft.

00:16:14.550 --> 00:16:16.500
Which risk strategy is this?

00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:22.000
Is it A, transfer, B, retention, C, reduction, or D, avoidance?

00:16:22.825 --> 00:16:24.100
Take a second.

00:16:24.875 --> 00:16:26.863
The answer is A, transfer.

00:16:26.863 --> 00:16:30.725
Buying insurance shifts the financial risk onto the insurer.

00:16:32.675 --> 00:16:36.775
Insurance written years ago may not fit the family's life today.

00:16:37.575 --> 00:16:43.038
Families change: they buy property, have children, hire staff and travel more.

00:16:43.038 --> 00:16:47.350
Your job is to track every policy and check twice a year whether the coverage still

00:16:47.350 --> 00:16:48.875
matches their life.

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:54.025
First, keep a secure policy tracker: type, insurer,

00:16:54.025 --> 00:16:58.600
policy number, who's covered, deductible, premium and renewal date.

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:02.150
Next, watch every renewal date, so nothing lapses.

00:17:02.150 --> 00:17:05.200
Then, compare what they have with what they now need.

00:17:07.150 --> 00:17:10.625
After that, at each review, ask: new property or car?

00:17:10.625 --> 00:17:12.063
Marriage or divorce?

00:17:12.063 --> 00:17:13.138
New dependents?

00:17:13.138 --> 00:17:14.338
Business changes?

00:17:14.338 --> 00:17:15.238
More wealth?

00:17:15.238 --> 00:17:17.425
Tell the broker about every yes.

00:17:17.425 --> 00:17:21.550
Finally, treat each yes as a gap to close, not a note for later.

00:17:22.325 --> 00:17:23.863
Try it on the Thornes.

00:17:23.863 --> 00:17:26.363
In the last six months they bought a lake house,

00:17:26.363 --> 00:17:30.450
hired a full-time nanny and started traveling abroad every quarter.

00:17:30.450 --> 00:17:32.250
Which gaps do you flag?

00:17:34.175 --> 00:17:35.063
Quick check.

00:17:35.063 --> 00:17:39.400
During the semi-annual risk review, which of these is a genuine trigger to revisit

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:40.500
coverage?

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:45.475
Is it A, a vendor changed their logo, B, the assistant took a vacation,

00:17:45.475 --> 00:17:49.262
C, net worth increased significantly since the last review,

00:17:49.262 --> 00:17:51.500
or D, the weather changed?

00:17:52.325 --> 00:17:53.600
Take a second.

00:17:54.375 --> 00:17:54.875
It's C.

00:17:54.700 --> 00:17:59.650
A meaningful change in net worth, property, vehicles or household staff should

00:17:59.650 --> 00:18:01.725
always prompt a coverage check.

00:18:03.675 --> 00:18:07.875
A confident-sounding request is not the same as a verified one.

00:18:08.650 --> 00:18:12.913
You see more private information than almost anyone around the executive,

00:18:12.913 --> 00:18:16.950
so information security is your job, not just IT's.

00:18:17.750 --> 00:18:22.075
First, only access and keep the sensitive information you actually need.

00:18:22.075 --> 00:18:26.425
Next, confirm who you're talking to before sharing anything sensitive by phone or

00:18:26.425 --> 00:18:27.400
email.

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:31.000
Then, use secure channels, never casual texts.

00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:34.475
Finally, know what to do if something leaks: stop it spreading,

00:18:34.475 --> 00:18:36.888
work out how much got out and escalate.

00:18:36.888 --> 00:18:38.350
Blame can wait.

00:18:39.125 --> 00:18:40.250
Think about this.

00:18:40.250 --> 00:18:44.725
An email that looks like it's from Elias's bank asks you to confirm his account

00:18:44.725 --> 00:18:49.400
details to clear a 'security flag.' It arrived at an odd time.

00:18:49.400 --> 00:18:51.450
What do you do before replying?

00:18:53.375 --> 00:18:57.550
Being the only person who knows how to do something isn't job security.

00:18:57.550 --> 00:18:58.775
It's a risk.

00:18:59.575 --> 00:19:04.188
Operational continuity means essential things keep happening even when you're sick,

00:19:04.188 --> 00:19:07.338
a system goes down or a key vendor fails.

00:19:07.338 --> 00:19:12.550
The SOPs, the binder and the contact list you've been building are all continuity

00:19:12.550 --> 00:19:13.600
tools.

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:18.500
First, list the tasks that would actually break if you were unreachable for 48

00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:19.525
hours.

00:19:19.525 --> 00:19:23.975
Next, write those processes down briefly, so someone else could do them.

00:19:23.975 --> 00:19:28.375
Then, have a backup contact for every critical vendor and system.

00:19:28.375 --> 00:19:32.000
Finally, create a simple 'if I'm unreachable' plan,

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:34.375
and make sure someone knows it exists.

00:19:35.150 --> 00:19:36.413
Think about this.

00:19:36.413 --> 00:19:40.550
A temp tries to follow the continuity plan you wrote six months ago.

00:19:40.550 --> 00:19:44.550
The alarm company number is wrong and two vendors have changed.

00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:47.450
What do you change about how you keep the plan current?

00:19:49.375 --> 00:19:53.762
Assume anything you write could one day be seen by people it wasn't meant for.

00:19:53.762 --> 00:19:58.712
Reputational damage spreads faster and lasts longer than most other problems,

00:19:58.712 --> 00:20:01.350
so your role is mostly preventive.

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:05.925
First, make discretion your default for anything that could go public.

00:20:05.925 --> 00:20:10.988
Next, look at guest lists, appearances and social posts with reputation in mind,

00:20:10.988 --> 00:20:12.700
not just logistics.

00:20:12.700 --> 00:20:15.800
Then, know who needs to hear first if something goes wrong,

00:20:15.800 --> 00:20:18.175
and who is allowed to speak publicly.

00:20:18.175 --> 00:20:21.925
Finally, control what gets shared, with whom and when.

00:20:22.700 --> 00:20:23.938
Think about this.

00:20:23.938 --> 00:20:28.388
A journalist contacts you directly, outside the firm's usual channels,

00:20:28.388 --> 00:20:32.250
asking for comment on a sensitive matter involving Elias.

00:20:32.250 --> 00:20:34.950
What do you do, and what do you deliberately avoid?

00:20:36.875 --> 00:20:40.825
Physical safety is the one risk where a gap can't be undone.

00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:45.625
This is about real, physical danger to the executive or family,

00:20:45.625 --> 00:20:49.175
whether traveling, at events or in daily life.

00:20:49.175 --> 00:20:53.900
Duty of care means knowing where someone is and being able to reach or help them.

00:20:55.850 --> 00:21:00.200
First, always know where the executive is during travel or high-profile events,

00:21:00.200 --> 00:21:02.025
without being intrusive.

00:21:02.025 --> 00:21:06.925
Next, before an unfamiliar or riskier trip, learn the local emergency numbers,

00:21:06.925 --> 00:21:09.775
the nearest hospital and any advisories.

00:21:09.775 --> 00:21:13.300
Then, work with security staff, not around them.

00:21:13.300 --> 00:21:17.800
Finally, keep emergency and basic medical information where you can find it in

00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:18.875
seconds.

00:21:19.650 --> 00:21:20.888
Try this one.

00:21:20.888 --> 00:21:24.725
Elias's daughter is traveling alone to a summer program abroad,

00:21:24.725 --> 00:21:27.938
and she's agreed to check in by text every evening.

00:21:27.938 --> 00:21:30.825
On day three, there's no message by midnight.

00:21:30.825 --> 00:21:33.650
What was agreed in advance, and what do you do now?

00:21:35.575 --> 00:21:40.462
You probably approve invoices, manage accounts or handle reimbursements,

00:21:40.462 --> 00:21:43.450
so financial controls are part of your job.

00:21:43.450 --> 00:21:48.050
And they protect you too: clear limits and records mean it's never your word against

00:21:48.050 --> 00:21:49.425
someone else's.

00:21:50.225 --> 00:21:54.275
First, know your approval limit in actual dollars before you need it.

00:21:54.275 --> 00:21:59.125
Next, require paperwork for every transaction, even the small ones.

00:21:59.125 --> 00:22:02.750
Then, reconcile recurring spending at least monthly.

00:22:02.750 --> 00:22:08.050
Finally, flag anything unusual straight away, even if you're not sure it's wrong.

00:22:08.800 --> 00:22:09.962
Try this one.

00:22:09.962 --> 00:22:13.938
A text from an unknown number says: 'It's Sarah, new phone.

00:22:13.938 --> 00:22:17.850
Please send $4,000 to this account for the kitchen contractor,

00:22:17.850 --> 00:22:21.325
I'm in a meeting.' What do you do in the next ten minutes?

00:22:23.225 --> 00:22:27.688
Processing each charge as it comes in isn't the same as reviewing them.

00:22:27.688 --> 00:22:31.800
Odd charges are much easier to spot when you look at a month together.

00:22:31.800 --> 00:22:35.462
It's the same reconciliation habit from financial controls,

00:22:35.462 --> 00:22:38.075
applied to the family's personal spending.

00:22:38.875 --> 00:22:42.025
First, set a regular review, usually monthly.

00:22:42.025 --> 00:22:45.238
Next, compare what was charged with what you expected:

00:22:45.238 --> 00:22:48.125
subscriptions, contracts and staff pay.

00:22:48.125 --> 00:22:52.175
Finally, flag anything unclear rather than assuming it's fine.

00:22:52.950 --> 00:22:54.212
Think about this.

00:22:54.212 --> 00:23:00.712
The monthly review shows a $450 charge at a jewelry store that nobody has mentioned.

00:23:00.712 --> 00:23:04.125
How do you raise it without implying anyone did something wrong?

00:23:06.050 --> 00:23:08.913
Section 5 of 6: Travel Risk.

00:23:08.913 --> 00:23:13.100
Managing risk before, during and after a trip, handling a travel claim,

00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:14.925
and duty of care abroad.

00:23:16.850 --> 00:23:22.450
Travel safety has three phases, before, during and after, and each has its own job.

00:23:23.225 --> 00:23:27.575
Before the trip, we confirm travel insurance and medical cover abroad,

00:23:27.575 --> 00:23:31.000
and for high-profile trips we register with the embassy.

00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:34.675
During it, the traveler has an emergency contact sheet,

00:23:34.675 --> 00:23:37.975
copies of key documents and safe Wi-Fi habits.

00:23:37.975 --> 00:23:42.613
And after it, we reconcile expenses and file any claims while the details are still

00:23:42.613 --> 00:23:43.575
fresh.

00:23:44.350 --> 00:23:46.588
Here's a situation to work through.

00:23:46.588 --> 00:23:50.512
Elias is in Mexico City and his phone has been stolen.

00:23:50.512 --> 00:23:55.025
What should already be on his printed emergency sheet, and who does he call first?

00:23:56.925 --> 00:24:00.038
When something goes wrong on a trip, the order is safety,

00:24:00.038 --> 00:24:02.638
evidence and never admitting fault.

00:24:02.638 --> 00:24:06.363
A travel claim isn't just paperwork; it's a risk event.

00:24:06.363 --> 00:24:11.150
You're the coordinator, the keeper of the documents and the link to the executive.

00:24:11.950 --> 00:24:16.475
First, make sure everyone is safe, and call emergency services if needed.

00:24:16.475 --> 00:24:21.050
Then, preserve evidence immediately: photos, damaged items,

00:24:21.050 --> 00:24:23.475
report numbers and witness details.

00:24:25.400 --> 00:24:28.675
After that, never admit fault on the executive's behalf,

00:24:28.675 --> 00:24:30.475
however it looks in the moment.

00:24:30.475 --> 00:24:35.250
Finally, only once things are safe, move on to the forms and the claim.

00:24:36.025 --> 00:24:37.238
Think about this.

00:24:37.238 --> 00:24:42.238
Elias's rental car is rear-ended in Lisbon, and the other driver insists it was his

00:24:42.238 --> 00:24:43.075
fault.

00:24:43.075 --> 00:24:44.875
He calls you from the roadside.

00:24:44.875 --> 00:24:47.675
What do you tell him to do, and in what order?

00:24:49.675 --> 00:24:53.625
Duty of care means knowing where a traveler is and being able to reach them if

00:24:53.625 --> 00:24:55.188
something goes wrong.

00:24:55.188 --> 00:24:58.588
International travel brings risks home travel doesn't:

00:24:58.588 --> 00:25:04.363
political unrest, weaker health systems, unfamiliar laws and language barriers.

00:25:04.363 --> 00:25:08.625
The riskier the place, the more planning has to happen before departure.

00:25:09.425 --> 00:25:14.200
First, check official travel advisories before booking, not just before leaving.

00:25:14.200 --> 00:25:18.850
Next, register the trip with the government's travel program where one exists.

00:25:18.850 --> 00:25:22.913
Then, confirm international health cover, and for riskier places,

00:25:22.913 --> 00:25:25.350
medical evacuation insurance.

00:25:25.350 --> 00:25:28.938
Finally, make a destination emergency card: embassy,

00:25:28.938 --> 00:25:32.875
hospital, local emergency numbers and a check-in schedule.

00:25:33.650 --> 00:25:35.938
Here's a situation to work through.

00:25:35.938 --> 00:25:41.450
Elias's daily check-in on an international trip is set for 8 p.m. local time.

00:25:41.450 --> 00:25:45.700
It's now 10 p.m. with no word, and his phone goes to voicemail.

00:25:45.700 --> 00:25:48.650
What was your agreed plan, and what do you do next?

00:25:50.575 --> 00:25:53.613
Section 6 of 6: Mid-Point Review.

00:25:53.613 --> 00:25:57.300
A one-on-one look at how the first half of the course has gone for you.

00:25:59.200 --> 00:26:03.588
We're halfway through, and this is the best moment to fix anything that still feels

00:26:03.588 --> 00:26:04.650
shaky.

00:26:04.650 --> 00:26:08.350
This review is a two-way conversation, not a verdict.

00:26:09.150 --> 00:26:13.150
First, come in with an honest view of which days and tools feel solid and which

00:26:13.150 --> 00:26:14.775
still feel shaky.

00:26:14.775 --> 00:26:18.125
Next, use the real data: Knowledge Check scores,

00:26:18.125 --> 00:26:21.100
Practice Lab history and roleplay results.

00:26:21.100 --> 00:26:24.550
Finally, leave with one specific focus for the second half,

00:26:24.550 --> 00:26:26.625
not a vague 'try harder.'

00:26:27.375 --> 00:26:28.863
Take a moment with this.

00:26:28.863 --> 00:26:31.263
Look honestly at Days 1 to 5.

00:26:31.263 --> 00:26:34.900
Which day or Practice Lab tool would you most like to revisit,

00:26:34.900 --> 00:26:37.500
and what exactly still feels unclear?

00:26:39.475 --> 00:26:40.863
Now it's your turn.

00:26:40.863 --> 00:26:44.350
In today's Practice Lab, the Household Risk Assessment,

00:26:44.350 --> 00:26:47.513
you'll classify the risks facing the Thorne family,

00:26:47.513 --> 00:26:50.688
match each one to the right risk-management strategy,

00:26:50.688 --> 00:26:55.713
build a real Home Binder section, and then handle a live crisis roleplay drawn from

00:26:55.713 --> 00:26:57.825
real household scenarios.

